# Task 7 Fix Round 2/5 — 2026-08-28

## Outcome and corrected prior claims

Both open Important findings in `task-7-rereview-1.md` are fixed in commit `35614fbc5edcbb4e0daa489f0f21233f5ee0cbce` (`fix: make PIT artifact writes race safe`). The historical Fix Round 1 paragraphs above were corrected in place: Round 1 had only pre-existing-link pathname coverage, and its leak-marker literals were never injected. This round supplies the actual post-check replacement race and boundary-injected omission proof. The separately deferred Minor was not touched, and none of the five already addressed findings was reopened.

Files changed:

- `core/pit_optimizer_artifacts.py`
  - Replaced check-then-path mutation with held directory capabilities for all direct create-only helpers and all incremental-store create/replace paths.
  - POSIX walks absolute and store-relative components with `O_DIRECTORY | O_NOFOLLOW` descriptors and performs final create, temporary create, unlink, and atomic replace relative to the held parent descriptor.
  - Windows opens each directory component with native `CreateFileW` using `FILE_FLAG_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT | FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS`, validates non-reparse `lstat` identity across handle acquisition, and deliberately omits `FILE_SHARE_DELETE` so the complete held chain cannot be renamed or replaced during a pathname mutation.
  - Preserved exclusive creation, file fsync, parent-directory durability, temporary cleanup, create-failure cleanup, store-root identity binding, and the exact closed artifact layout.
- `tests/test_pit_optimizer_loop.py`
  - Added a deterministic post-check/pre-mutation replacement race for create-only JSON, replaceable JSON, and replaceable diff.
  - Injected three distinct synthetic markers into the fake raw-provider envelope, approval input, and ignored candidate input; proved each boundary was exercised and that every persisted artifact plus the public result omitted all three markers.

## Finding 5 — race-safe create and replace mutations

Focused command:

```text
python -B -m pytest -p no:cacheprovider --no-cov -q tests/test_pit_optimizer_loop.py -k post_check_path_replacement_race
```

RED against the pathname writer:

```text
FFF                                                                      [100%]
3 failed, 63 deselected, 1 warning in 0.26s
```

The deterministic hook ran after the existing parent checks and immediately before `Path.open("xb")` or `tempfile.mkstemp`. On this Windows host, all three unguarded directory renames succeeded; the create-only artifact and both replacement payloads were written into the injected replacement directory. This was the expected security failure, not a fixture error.

GREEN after held-capability mutation:

```text
...                                                                      [100%]
3 passed, 63 deselected, 1 warning in 0.18s
```

On Windows the same three replacement attempts now fail while the native component handles are held without delete sharing; the intended in-root artifacts complete with the exact expected bytes, no detached directory appears, and each outside/replacement target remains absent or byte-for-byte unchanged. On POSIX the same test hooks the final relative `os.open`; the implementation uses held no-follow directory descriptors and revalidates identity around mutation, so a successful pathname replacement cannot redirect create/unlink/replace into the installed link or directory.

The older pre-existing directory-symlink test remains an honest capability test: it skips on this Windows token because directory symlink creation is unavailable. The new ordinary path-replacement race does not require that privilege and executed on all three mutation paths here; it is not skipped.

